.TH SURVEILLANCE 1 "2026-09-13" "0.10.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
surveillance \- Synology Surveillance Station client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B surveillance
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-file [\fB=\fPPATH]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B surveillance
is a native GTK4 desktop client for Synology Surveillance Station.
It connects to a Synology NAS via its REST API to provide live camera views,
recording playback, PTZ control, snapshots, events/alerts, and home mode management.
.SH FEATURES
.TP
.B Live View
View live camera streams in configurable grid layouts (1x1, 2x2, 3x3, 4x4),
selected from the grid button in the header bar.
Each layout remembers its own camera arrangement independently.
A single slot is emptied from the camera sidebar, the whole layout from the
grid menu after a confirmation prompt.
Scroll the mouse wheel over a slot to zoom in, centered on the cursor;
click and drag to pan. Zoom resets when switching layouts or leaving the
page.
Streams are muted by default; hovering a slot reveals a toolbar with
mute/volume (remembered per camera) and a Snapshot button.
Audio reaches the player over the RTSP-family protocols
.RB ( rtsp ,
.BR rtsp_over_http ,
.BR multicast ,
.BR direct ),
or over the default
.B auto/websocket
protocol when the camera's audio codec is PCMU or AAC (muxed in via
ffmpeg); otherwise the mute button stays greyed out until the protocol is
changed (or DSM reports a different codec) by right-clicking the camera
in the sidebar. A camera with no audio track gets no mute button at all.
A muxed camera that stops sending audio for three seconds while its video
keeps arriving keeps playing without sound until its stream is restarted,
rather than holding up its own video.
Cameras with a speaker also show a push-to-talk microphone button — tap to
start talking, tap again to stop.
Video is rendered via mpv with hardware acceleration.
The client sends a periodic keepalive to hold each WebSocket session open;
if a session is ever interrupted anyway, the slot reconnects on the same
pipe transparently, with no visible interruption. A camera reported
disabled or disconnected shows
an
.RI ( offline )
placeholder instead of freezing, and a stream that stops responding
mid-session shows
.RI ( "stream lost" )
or
.RI ( "attempting reconnect" )
while retrying. Both recover automatically once the camera is reachable
again.
A slot keeps its stream when a layout switch leaves its camera in the
same position, so a single feed that has drifted behind or stopped
moving is restarted from
.B Reload
in the slot's right-click menu, without disturbing the other slots; a
slot showing recorded video reloads at the same moment rather than
returning to live.
.TP
.B Live View Timeline
A shared timeline strip below the grid, toggled from the header bar
(persists across restarts). Scroll or use the zoom buttons to zoom the
time scale (centered on the cursor), click-and-drag to pan; a thin blue
border marks which slot's camera the timeline is currently tracking
\(em it follows the last-clicked slot, defaults to the upper-left slot
on layout load, and fades after 10 seconds of no activity anywhere in
the window. Hovering the ruler shows a small preview thumbnail of that
camera at the hovered time, with its date/time overlaid. In History
mode, a date/time bubble follows the marker as it moves. Below the
ruler, a recording-presence bar shows two rows: where the tracked
slot's own camera has a recording, and where any camera in the current
layout does.
.IP
Clicking a point on the ruler switches every slot in the current
layout into History mode, each playing its own camera's recorded video
from that time; a
.B Live
button returns every slot to the real-time stream. Picking a different
camera into a slot already in History mode keeps playing recorded
video, for the newly picked camera, at the same point in time, rather
than dropping back to live; switching grid layout always returns to
live first. Pan/tilt/zoom, focus, preset, patrol, and push-to-talk are
unavailable while a slot shows recorded video (mute/volume and
Snapshot remain available). Jump \(+-10s buttons seek every slot in
the current layout the same way clicking the ruler does. A
.B Pause
button freezes every active slot in place \(em a live slot just
freezes locally without leaving Live mode, while a History slot asks
DSM to actually stop sending, so the gap behind wall clock grows for
as long as it stays paused; resuming a History slot paused for under
10 seconds lands at least 10 seconds behind live rather than right at
the edge. A playback-speed dropdown (History mode only) offers 1/8x
through 100x plus a Fwd/Rev direction toggle, applied to every active
History slot at once; it resets to 1x/Fwd whenever a slot returns to
Live or the layout switches. The higher speeds are greyed out on the
larger layouts, since DSM really does send that many more frames per
second and every slot has to decode them: by default, 1x1 offers the
full range, 2x2 stops at 16x, 3x3 at 8x, and 4x4 at 4x (adjustable from
the Settings page; 1x is always offered, whatever the budget). Real motion/alarm events are overlaid on
the same two presence rows as orange markers.
.B Previous event
and
.B Next event
jump to the nearest one on either side of the current position, across
every camera in the layout; Previous stays available in Live mode
(dropping into History first, like Back 10s), while Next is
History-only, like Forward 10s. Events within 10 seconds of wall clock
are skipped, since a seek that close would just be clamped back to the
live edge anyway. A calendar button opens a date/time picker, its own
days marked and any day without a recording (across the current
layout) refused, and its
.B Jump
button greyed out until the exact date/time selected falls within a
real recording; jumping there re-centers the timeline at its default
zoom, while Back/Forward 10s and Previous/Next event instead just pan
(at whatever zoom is already set) if their own target would otherwise
land off-screen. A
.B Filter events
button narrows the presence bar's event markers and Previous/Next
event navigation down to chosen event types (Any or All of a
multi-select, decoded per camera brand). Opening it scans each layout
camera's full recording history for the types it has ever produced (a
persisted, incrementally-updated cache, so only the first scan and any
time elapsed since the last one cost real seconds), showing a
per-camera progress checklist meanwhile. A
.B Download
button opens a popup with a camera picker (only cameras currently
assigned to a slot in the layout, since some may be empty, and
defaulting to the tracked slot's own camera), above two tabs: Quick
Save, whose three one-click buttons immediately prompt for a save
location and download around the tracked slot's current position \(em
.B "Download last 1/2/5 min"
up to it in Live mode, or
.B "Download \-1/2/5 to +1/2/5 min"
centered on it in History mode, since both directions are already
available once paused on a moment of interest \(em and Custom Save,
with Start/End date-time fields (defaulting to a short clip ending at
the same point) for an exact range.
.TP
.B Camera Sidebar
Lists the cameras alphabetically and switches between pages.
The panel button in the header bar hides or shows it, and that choice is
remembered across restarts.
.TP
.B Recording Playback
Browse, play back, and download recordings filtered by camera and date.
Quick date presets (Today, Yesterday, Last 24 hrs, Last 7 days) for one-click filtering,
plus advanced search by camera(s) and custom time range. Reset button clears all filters.
Active filter summary always visible. Per-event thumbnails are shown for each recording.
The playback window supports scroll-to-zoom (centered on the cursor) and
click-and-drag panning, same as Live View.
.TP
.B PTZ Control
Control pan-tilt-zoom cameras with Pan/Tilt, Zoom, Focus, Preset, and Patrol
controls, in the same per-slot hover toolbar as Live View's audio controls.
Picking a patrol asks Surveillance Station to run that saved route; the NAS
drives the camera, so it keeps going after you switch cameras or quit.
Routes are created and edited in Surveillance Station itself, and there is
no stop control, the same as Synology's own clients.
.TP
.B Snapshots
Browse saved snapshots, filter by camera and time range, view, download,
and delete. A snapshot can also be taken from a Live View slot's right-click
menu, which saves it to the snapshot database and offers a local copy.
The full-size viewer supports scroll-to-zoom (centered on the cursor) and
click-and-drag panning.
.TP
.B Time Lapse
Browse, play back, download, and manage Smart Time Lapse recordings.
.TP
.B Events & Alerts
Browse real events decoded from each camera's own detected categories
(motion, audio, tampering, person/vehicle/pet, and more, brand-dependent)
with their type and time, filtered by event type, camera, and time range.
Advanced search can match Any or All of several selected event types at
once. Camera alerts appear in the notification popover with an unread
badge.
.TP
.B Home Mode
Toggle Surveillance Station home mode from the header bar.
.TP
.B Two-Factor Authentication
MFA/OTP login support. Prompts for a 6-digit authenticator code when 2FA is
enabled and optionally registers as a trusted device to skip OTP on future
logins.
.TP
.B License Management
View, add, and delete camera licenses. Supports online and offline activation.
.TP
.B Settings
Tune the demuxer cache sizes used for each streaming profile (plain RTSP,
WebSocket muxed-audio, and silent WebSocket), the extra buffer added
automatically at high History playback speeds, the shared demuxer byte
cap, and the speed-times-active-slots budget the Live View timeline's
History speed dropdown enforces, plus an on-screen readout of cache
depth/target/effective speed for diagnosing buffering. Each setting has
its own reset-to-default button, plus one that resets everything on the
page at once. Changes apply to the next stream that starts (the
on-screen readout takes effect immediately) and persist across restarts.
.TP
.B About
Shows the version, license, and repository links, and checks the GitHub
releases page once per login for a newer version, marking the About entry
when one is available.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable debug logging to stderr.
Passwords, session tokens, usernames, one-time codes, and credentials
embedded in stream URLs are redacted from the output, including inside
exception tracebacks.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-file [\fB=\fPPATH]
Also write logs to a file, at the same redacted content and level
(WARNING, or DEBUG if \-\-debug is also given) as stderr, plus the two
things that reach stderr without going through the log at all: the
traceback of an uncaught exception, on the main thread or a worker one,
and the messages GTK and GLib print themselves. With
.I PATH
given, writes there, truncated on every run. Without it, writes to an
automatically named file under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/surveillance-station/logs/ ,
or
.I ~/.local/state/surveillance-station/logs/
if
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is not set, and marks it complete on a clean exit (window close,
Ctrl-C, or SIGTERM) by creating a same-named
.I .complete
sentinel file alongside it \(em an abnormal exit (a crash, or the
process being killed) leaves the log without one. The next run that
also passes a bare
.B \-\-log\-file
deletes every previous log marked complete this way along with its
sentinel; one left over from an abnormal exit is kept for inspection,
so disk usage only grows across repeated crashes, not from ordinary
use. The automatically named file is created mode 0600.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored in TOML format at:
.PP
.I ~/.config/surveillance-station/config.toml
.PP
Connection profiles define NAS host, port, HTTPS, and SSL verification settings.
Credentials are stored securely via the system keyring. When two-factor
authentication is enabled, trusted device tokens are stored per profile in the
config file.
.PP
The
.B [general]
section stores the theme, the polling intervals, the snapshot directory,
.IR sidebar_visible ,
which records whether the camera sidebar is shown,
.IR timeline_visible ,
which records whether the Live View timeline strip is shown, and
.IR dismissed_update_version ,
the release tag whose update notice has been dismissed.
.PP
.IR poll_interval_cameras ", " poll_interval_alerts " and " poll_interval_homemode
are in seconds and default to 30, 30 and 60. The minimum is 5: a smaller or
non-numeric value is reported in the log and replaced, since 0 would poll the
NAS continuously.
.IR poll_interval_cameras
also paces recovery: a live view slot whose stream gave up is retried on the
next camera poll, so it is the interval between one attempt and the next.
.PP
The
.B [session]
section stores the grid layout, active page, camera assignments, and recording
search filters (including time presets). It is updated automatically so the
application restores the previous viewing session on restart. Critical changes
are flushed to disk immediately for crash resilience.
.PP
The
.B [camera_protocols]
section selects the stream protocol per camera (auto, websocket,
mjpeg, rtsp_over_http, rtsp, multicast, direct).
.B auto
is the same as
.BR websocket ;
there is no fallback between protocols, so pick one explicitly if
WebSocket does not work for a camera.
.IR websocket
uses a WebSocket stream bridged to mpv via an in-memory pipe, muxing in
real audio via ffmpeg when the camera's audio codec is PCMU or AAC.
When set to
.IR direct ,
the URL from
.B [camera_overrides]
is used instead of Synology's RTSP proxy.
These settings can be configured by right-clicking a camera in the sidebar.
.PP
The
.B [camera_volume]
and
.B [camera_muted]
sections hold the Live View volume (0-100) and mute state per camera, as set
from the slot's hover toolbar. Cameras start muted.
.PP
The
.B [event_type_history]
section caches which event types each camera has ever produced, as
discovered by the Live View timeline's
.B Filter events
popover. Each camera's entry holds
.IR types ,
a list of (event type flag, reserved) pairs, and
.IR checked_until ,
the unix time its recording history has been scanned up to, so a later
open only scans forward from there rather than repeating the whole
history. Written automatically; deleting a camera's entry forces a full
rescan the next time the popover is opened.
.PP
The
.B [setting_overrides]
and
.B [setting_overrides_bool]
sections hold every value changed from its default on the
.B Settings
page (player cache sizes, the demuxer byte cap, the on-screen cache/speed
readout, and the History speed dropdown's speed-times-slots budget), split
by value type since TOML distinguishes numbers from booleans. Written by
the Settings page itself; use its own reset buttons rather than
hand-editing these.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit the application.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B System (Linux)
gtk4, libmpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, gir1.2-gtk-4.0
.TP
.B System (FreeBSD)
gtk4, mpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, py311-gobject3, py311-cairo
.TP
.B System (OpenBSD)
gtk4, mpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, py3-gobject3, py3-cairo
.TP
.B Python
PyGObject (>=3.50), httpx, python-mpv, PyOpenGL, keyring, tomli-w, websockets,
sounddevice, cryptography
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/surveillance-station/config.toml
Application configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/surveillance-station/snapshots/
Default snapshot download directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SURVEILLANCE_AO
Audio output driver handed to mpv, taking any name
.B mpv \-\-ao
accepts, for example
.IR pulse ,
.I pipewire
or
.IR alsa .
Left unset, mpv chooses for itself. The AppImage sets it to
.I pulse
where it has to run its own bundled PipeWire library, whose output driver
would crash there; setting it explicitly overrides that.
.TP
.B SURVEILLANCE_MPV_OPTS
Further options for the embedded mpv player, separated by whitespace and
written as
.I name=value
the way
.I mpv.conf
spells them, or a bare
.I name
for a flag, as on mpv's command line. Quote a value containing whitespace
as in a shell. They are applied after the client's own options and
replace them by name, the driver chosen through
.B SURVEILLANCE_AO
included. A name mpv does not know stops the player from starting, and
the log names it. For example
.B hwdec=nvdec hwdec-extra-frames=12
picks NVIDIA decoding and enlarges its surface pool; see
.I TROUBLESHOOTING.md
for when that is needed.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
See
.I TROUBLESHOOTING.md
in the source repository for common problems and fixes
(HTTP 502, playback failures, download errors, AppImage audio crashes,
Ubuntu 24.04 / AppImage notes).
